{{also|Tuba|túba|tuba'}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
[[File:Tuba.JPG|thumb|A tuba ''(sense 1)''.]]

Borrowed in the 19th century from {{bor|en|fr|tuba||tuba}},<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary|pos=n}}</ref> {{bor|en|de|Tuba||tuba}}, originally {{m|de|Baß-Tuba|lit=bass tuba}}, or {{bor|en|it|tuba||tuba}},<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''n.''{{sup|1}}|noformat=1|id=1163453201}}</ref> from {{der|en|la|tuba||tube, trumpet, military trumpet}}. The Latin term is of obscure ultimate origin, but is possibly connected to {{m|la|tībia||shinbone, reed-pipe}}; see there.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtjuː.bə/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtu.bə/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-uk-tuba.ogg|a=UK}}
* {{rhymes|en|uːbə|s=2}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A large [[brass]] [[musical instrument]], usually in the [[bass]] range, played through a vibration of the lips upon the [[mouthpiece]] and fingering of the keys.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1990|author=Thomas D. Rossing|title=The Science of Sound|page=230
|passage=One version of the large '''tuba''', popular in marching bands, is called a sousaphone in honor of bandsman John Philip Sousa.}}
# A large [[reed stop]] in [[organ]]s.

