# A type of [[Roman]] [[military]] [[trumpet]], distinct from the modern tuba.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1968|author=Roger Bragard; Ferdinand J{{quote-gloss|oseph}} de Hen|chapter_tlr=[[w:Bill Hopkins (composer)|Bill Hopkins]]|chapter=Wind Instruments|title=Musical Instruments in Art and History|series=A Studio Book|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Viking Press|section=part II (The Middle Ages)|page=60|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/musicalinstrumen0000unse_u0n8/page/60/mode/1up|lccn=68-15484|oclc=336611|passage=From the eleventh century onwards, the trumpets, which had till then been modelled on the Roman '''tubae''' with their straightforward and somewhat rough sound, began to grow longer and thinner as their bells widened.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1986|author=Raymond Buniak|title=A Twentieth-Century Treatise on the Trombone|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=GpsIAQAAMAAJ&q=tubae|location=[Bartlett, Ill.]|publisher=Raymond Buniak|oclc=15252772|passage=This depicts trumpet blowing angels gripping instruments, of the Roman '''tubae''' variety, halfway down with the right hand, the left hand covering their mouth and the mouthpiece.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1994|month=April|author=w:Stephan Grundy|chapter=Along the Rhine|title=Rhinegold|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Bantam Books|section=book III (The Death of Athelings)|page=451|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/rhinegold00grun/page/451/mode/1up|isbn=0-553-09545-5|passage=As he drew nearer to Worms, he heard the brassy blare of Roman '''tubae''' from the ruddy sandstone walls of the city. }}

